Read the window colour from QPalette::Window in the Qt 6 style

The Qt 5 style reads QPalette::Background for the window colour.
Background is the deprecated name of QPalette::Window. Commit 52d0de6a3
replaced the deprecated names, and put QPalette::Base in the place of
Background. Base is the colour of a text entry. The Qt 6 style therefore
builds the window shade ladder m_backgroundCols from the colour of a
text entry.

The ladder gives the ground of a popup menu and the ground of a menu bar
that the darken option shades. It also gives the background of a scroll
bar and the background role that unpolish() puts back. The fill behind a
radio button in an OpenOffice window comes from the ladder as well. A
colour scheme that gives the window and the text entry different colours
shows each of these in the entry colour.

polish() sets QPalette::Disabled with two equal statements. Both must
read the active window colour, and both read the active entry colour.
The second statement must also write the disabled window colour, and
writes the disabled entry colour again. QtCurve therefore leaves the
disabled window colour of the theme in place.

exportQt() writes the palette of the user to ~/.qt/qtrc for a Qt 3
program. Its array of colour roles lost the Window entry, so each
[Palette] key holds fifteen colours. A Qt 3 reader takes the list by
position. It therefore reads the shadow colour as the window colour, and
finds no colour for the last role. The entry returns to the array, after
Base.

Every site now names the role that the Qt 5 style names.

The change sits on the branch work/qt6-window-palette of the local tree.
The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !16 on
system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

